WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.078 --> 00:00:01.548
Stakes are the reason that audiences

00:00:01.548 --> 00:00:02.908
become invested in your story.

00:00:03.328 --> 00:00:04.728
Stakes are the things that make an

00:00:04.728 --> 00:00:06.798
audience worry or wonder, makes them

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:08.487
curious, makes them root for you,

00:00:08.728 --> 00:00:10.198
makes them hope for your demise.

00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:12.488
Stakes are essential to a story.

00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:14.798
They are the things that make people

00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:16.717
want to hear the end of the story.

00:00:16.717 --> 00:00:17.927
They're the things that drive

00:00:17.957 --> 00:00:19.828
people to the end of the story.

00:00:19.988 --> 00:00:21.678
There are several types of stakes

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:22.758
that you can deploy in a story,

00:00:22.768 --> 00:00:24.128
and all can be very powerful.

00:00:24.178 --> 00:00:25.808
The most important stake of all

00:00:25.848 --> 00:00:27.008
is what I call the elephant.

00:00:28.668 --> 00:00:29.587
I call it the elephant

00:00:29.587 --> 00:00:30.607
because it's enormous.

00:00:30.778 --> 00:00:32.518
It's enormously important to a story.

00:00:32.908 --> 00:00:35.027
The elephant is essentially the initial

00:00:35.148 --> 00:00:36.808
signpost to the audience that says,

00:00:37.008 --> 00:00:38.768
"This is what my story is about, at

00:00:38.768 --> 00:00:40.808
least for now." In a movie, we don't

00:00:40.808 --> 00:00:42.288
really need an elephant because what

00:00:42.288 --> 00:00:44.018
we have today is movie trailers.

00:00:44.088 --> 00:00:45.198
When we go into a movie, we

00:00:45.198 --> 00:00:46.418
kinda know what we're gonna see.

00:00:46.797 --> 00:00:48.168
If we're going into a Mission Impossible

00:00:48.168 --> 00:00:49.638
movie, we know what the stakes are.

00:00:49.788 --> 00:00:51.067
The world is in danger and

00:00:51.068 --> 00:00:53.268
Tom Cruise must save the day.

00:00:53.508 --> 00:00:54.508
When we walk into a romantic

00:00:54.518 --> 00:00:55.618
comedy, we know what the stakes

00:00:55.618 --> 00:00:56.598
are, what the elephant is.

00:00:56.628 --> 00:00:58.298
It is two people are not in love.

00:00:58.448 --> 00:00:59.368
Will they be in love by

00:00:59.368 --> 00:00:59.948
the end of the movie?

00:00:59.948 --> 00:01:00.888
Those are the stakes.

00:01:01.318 --> 00:01:02.908
But when we're speaking, telling

00:01:02.908 --> 00:01:04.988
a story, delivering a sales pitch,

00:01:04.988 --> 00:01:06.378
speaking at a conference, delivering a

00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:08.538
keynote, nobody knows really what we're

00:01:08.538 --> 00:01:10.427
going to say as we open our mouths.

00:01:10.758 --> 00:01:11.888
We need to give them a reason

00:01:11.888 --> 00:01:13.328
right away to be invested in that

00:01:13.328 --> 00:01:14.738
story with what I call an elephant.

00:01:17.824 --> 00:01:19.614
Standing in the playground behind

00:01:19.614 --> 00:01:21.824
my school beside this enormous pile

00:01:21.824 --> 00:01:24.564
of leaves, and this tiny little hand

00:01:24.574 --> 00:01:26.554
comes out of the leaves with a silver

00:01:26.554 --> 00:01:29.024
metallic object in it, and the boy

00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:30.504
attached to the hand, he says, "Look,

00:01:30.504 --> 00:01:33.744
Mr. Dix, a spoon." And it, it's a spoon.

00:01:33.744 --> 00:01:35.314
It's a ordinary kitchen spoon,

00:01:35.314 --> 00:01:36.734
probably fell out of a lunchbox

00:01:36.803 --> 00:01:38.984
earlier that day or 10 years ago.

00:01:39.594 --> 00:01:43.274
But I say, "No, Jamie, that's the spoon

00:01:43.274 --> 00:01:46.264
of power." And now that I've declared

00:01:46.264 --> 00:01:48.304
it to be the spoon of power, I must

00:01:48.314 --> 00:01:50.404
have it because that is who I am.

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:52.674
And Jamie knows this.

00:01:52.674 --> 00:01:54.320
I can see it on the look in his face,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.394
and he knows that I know it, and I

00:01:56.394 --> 00:01:58.094
know that he knows that I know it,

00:01:58.274 --> 00:01:59.684
and all of this is true 'cause he's

00:01:59.684 --> 00:02:01.494
running before he says anything else.

00:02:01.904 --> 00:02:03.644
And the 74 kids that I'm supposed

00:02:03.644 --> 00:02:05.743
to be monitoring closely are lost

00:02:05.774 --> 00:02:07.834
to me, and the only thing I focus

00:02:07.834 --> 00:02:09.373
on is a little redheaded boy with

00:02:09.373 --> 00:02:11.844
a spoon that I must now have.

00:02:12.194 --> 00:02:14.444
And we run across the field, and we

00:02:14.444 --> 00:02:15.834
climb up the slide and through the

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:18.014
tubes, and 17 minutes later, the bell

00:02:18.014 --> 00:02:20.594
rings, and that little boy still has

00:02:20.594 --> 00:02:22.823
the spoon, and I can't believe it.

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:23.714
The spoon of power.

00:02:23.853 --> 00:02:24.904
It's a very similar

00:02:24.924 --> 00:02:25.874
elephant to the beginning.

00:02:26.163 --> 00:02:27.894
A spoon has made an appearance

00:02:27.924 --> 00:02:29.393
in a little boy's hand, and

00:02:29.403 --> 00:02:31.264
Matt must now have that spoon.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:32.984
Will he get the spoon?

00:02:33.464 --> 00:02:34.914
That is the elephant that I present

00:02:34.924 --> 00:02:36.064
at the beginning of the story.

00:02:36.214 --> 00:02:37.354
It's actually the elephant or the

00:02:37.384 --> 00:02:38.874
stake that carries us through much

00:02:38.874 --> 00:02:40.184
of the story until he eventually

00:02:40.184 --> 00:02:41.504
puts it on a chain around his neck,

00:02:41.814 --> 00:02:43.584
and then the elephant changes.

00:02:45.043 --> 00:02:48.653
It's it's 1991, and I'm 20 years old,

00:02:49.194 --> 00:02:51.144
and I'm driving home from the very

00:02:51.144 --> 00:02:53.624
first booty call of my entire life.

00:02:54.154 --> 00:02:57.384
And I am excited because I don't know

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:59.864
that this is going to also be the very

00:02:59.864 --> 00:03:02.394
last booty call of my entire life.

00:03:03.363 --> 00:03:05.314
And then it's in that moment of excitement

00:03:05.504 --> 00:03:08.724
that the right front tire of my 1976 Chevy

00:03:08.724 --> 00:03:11.084
Malibu blows out, but it doesn't deflate.

00:03:11.103 --> 00:03:12.274
It disintegrates.

00:03:12.464 --> 00:03:14.534
It throws rober- rubber on the road in

00:03:14.534 --> 00:03:16.374
a way that I didn't think was possible,

00:03:16.583 --> 00:03:18.914
and it takes everything I can to get the

00:03:19.103 --> 00:03:21.704
car over to the shoulder and to a stop.

00:03:22.053 --> 00:03:22.534
And it's 1991.

00:03:23.853 --> 00:03:25.034
I don't have a cell phone,

00:03:25.394 --> 00:03:26.584
so I do the only thing I can.

00:03:26.584 --> 00:03:27.474
I start hiking up the

00:03:27.474 --> 00:03:28.504
road to look for help.

00:03:29.214 --> 00:03:32.004
Seven hours later, after giving all

00:03:32.004 --> 00:03:33.864
of my money to a half-naked mountain

00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:36.393
man named Winston, I have a bald

00:03:36.404 --> 00:03:38.194
tire on my car, and I'm heading

00:03:38.194 --> 00:03:40.034
home when I look at the instrument

00:03:40.034 --> 00:03:42.233
panel, and I see that I have no gas.

00:03:42.694 --> 00:03:44.103
And all of the money that I

00:03:44.114 --> 00:03:46.044
have is in Winston's hand.

00:03:46.474 --> 00:03:47.914
And it's 1991.

00:03:47.914 --> 00:03:49.103
I don't have a credit card.

00:03:49.164 --> 00:03:50.454
I don't have an ATM card.

00:03:50.674 --> 00:03:52.724
I don't even have a checking account.

00:03:53.583 --> 00:03:55.344
And so I take the next exit, and I

00:03:55.344 --> 00:03:57.234
roll my car into a Getty station.

00:03:57.874 --> 00:03:59.073
And as I park the car,

00:03:59.114 --> 00:04:01.323
I'm angry, and I'm sad.

00:04:01.864 --> 00:04:03.364
I'm 20 years old.

00:04:03.584 --> 00:04:05.154
I'm a McDonald's manager.

00:04:05.464 --> 00:04:08.554
I make $7.25 an hour, and I'm

00:04:08.554 --> 00:04:10.184
the richest person I know.

00:04:11.064 --> 00:04:12.204
My mother is living on

00:04:12.204 --> 00:04:13.514
welfare with my sister.

00:04:14.122 --> 00:04:16.382
My father's left 10 years ago, and the

00:04:16.392 --> 00:04:18.632
only friend that I have who even has

00:04:18.672 --> 00:04:20.762
a credit card or a car that can make

00:04:20.762 --> 00:04:22.542
this 100-mile trip to New Hampshire

00:04:22.832 --> 00:04:24.592
is off on some college weekend,

00:04:24.592 --> 00:04:25.942
and I can't get in touch with him.

00:04:26.342 --> 00:04:28.492
And this was not the plan for my life.

00:04:28.522 --> 00:04:30.672
I was not supposed to be this alone.

00:04:31.462 --> 00:04:32.532
And so I go into the Getty

00:04:32.532 --> 00:04:34.262
station to beg for gas.

00:04:34.672 --> 00:04:37.142
$8 worth of gas is all I need 'cause

00:04:37.142 --> 00:04:40.982
it's 1991 and gas is 85 cents a gallon.

00:04:41.772 --> 00:04:43.422
And the kid behind the counter

00:04:43.462 --> 00:04:44.632
will not give it to me.

00:04:44.942 --> 00:04:46.472
He doesn't wanna lose his job, and

00:04:46.472 --> 00:04:48.592
I offer everything, and he says no.

00:04:48.962 --> 00:04:49.992
So I go back to my car.

00:04:49.992 --> 00:04:51.112
My plan is to wait for

00:04:51.112 --> 00:04:52.142
the next shift change.

00:04:52.402 --> 00:04:53.202
Maybe someone more

00:04:53.202 --> 00:04:54.502
sympathetic will take over.

00:04:54.792 --> 00:04:56.552
But as I climb into my car, I see

00:04:56.552 --> 00:04:58.162
my McDonald's uniform crumpled

00:04:58.162 --> 00:05:00.262
in the back, and I have an idea.

00:05:00.362 --> 00:05:02.572
The initial elephant, the question, the

00:05:02.572 --> 00:05:04.542
curiosity, the reason you wanna be driven

00:05:04.542 --> 00:05:06.521
home to the end of the story is Matt

00:05:06.531 --> 00:05:09.152
has a flat tire, and he has no money.

00:05:09.522 --> 00:05:10.712
How will he get home?

00:05:11.092 --> 00:05:12.062
That is the stake.

00:05:12.082 --> 00:05:13.692
That is the elephant at the beginning

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:14.992
of the story that will grab your

00:05:14.992 --> 00:05:16.492
attention and cause you to wonder,

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:17.812
how will this problem be solved?

00:05:17.982 --> 00:05:19.162
If you're a problem solver, the

00:05:19.162 --> 00:05:20.221
kind of person that likes to puzzle

00:05:20.232 --> 00:05:21.422
things out, you might have actually

00:05:21.422 --> 00:05:22.962
started to think about solutions

00:05:22.972 --> 00:05:24.981
for me, solutions on my behalf.

00:05:25.072 --> 00:05:26.442
Something that happened 30 years ago

00:05:26.442 --> 00:05:27.802
you're worried about now and wondering

00:05:27.832 --> 00:05:28.972
how I was gonna get out of it.

00:05:29.222 --> 00:05:30.272
The goal of an elephant is to

00:05:30.272 --> 00:05:32.232
give an audience a reason to be

00:05:32.232 --> 00:05:33.792
immediately invested in the story.

00:05:33.892 --> 00:05:35.712
How will Matt get out of New Hampshire?

00:05:35.722 --> 00:05:36.962
An hour later, I'm in a

00:05:36.962 --> 00:05:38.222
residential neighborhood about

00:05:38.222 --> 00:05:39.651
a mile from the Getty station.

00:05:40.071 --> 00:05:41.842
I'm on the porch of a red brick

00:05:41.842 --> 00:05:43.982
house, and I'm knocking on the door.

00:05:44.252 --> 00:05:46.022
I'm wearing my McDonald's uniform, the

00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:48.282
blue shirt, the blue pants, the blue tie.

00:05:49.122 --> 00:05:50.922
The door opens, and there's a man

00:05:50.922 --> 00:05:52.412
standing in front of me, and I know at

00:05:52.412 --> 00:05:55.502
that moment that this is a bad idea.

00:05:56.321 --> 00:05:58.082
But it's 3:00 on a Sunday

00:05:58.082 --> 00:06:00.062
afternoon, and I'm on his porch in

00:06:00.062 --> 00:06:02.122
a McDonald's uniform, and the only

00:06:02.122 --> 00:06:04.022
way to get off is to go forward.

00:06:04.442 --> 00:06:07.572
So I say to him, "My name is Matt.

00:06:08.022 --> 00:06:09.612
I'm collecting money for the Ronald

00:06:09.622 --> 00:06:12.542
McDonald Children's Charities. Can

00:06:12.542 --> 00:06:15.542
you please make a donation?" And this

00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:18.161
man stares at me like Stonehenge.

00:06:18.162 --> 00:06:19.272
He does not move.

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:21.972
He's 50, but he might as well be 500.

00:06:21.982 --> 00:06:24.052
He is all of the wisdom and knowledge

00:06:24.052 --> 00:06:25.932
of the world wrapped into one man,

00:06:25.962 --> 00:06:27.662
and I know he knows I'm lying.

00:06:27.932 --> 00:06:29.092
And the next words that come

00:06:29.092 --> 00:06:31.132
out of my mouth I don't expect.

00:06:31.321 --> 00:06:34.692
I say, "My mom died of cancer when I was

00:06:34.692 --> 00:06:38.302
young- … and my sister has cancer now,

00:06:38.712 --> 00:06:40.902
and I'm just trying to do what I can to

00:06:40.902 --> 00:06:44.472
help." And he looks at me and points and

00:06:44.472 --> 00:06:46.942
says, "You stay right there." And he goes

00:06:46.942 --> 00:06:48.722
back into the house and closes the door.

00:06:48.752 --> 00:06:51.432
And I know that he's calling the police,

00:06:51.772 --> 00:06:53.652
and I'm going to get arrested for stealing

00:06:53.652 --> 00:06:55.452
money from McDonald's, which ironically

00:06:55.452 --> 00:06:57.741
two years later will actually happen.

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:01.118
But not on this day.

00:07:01.858 --> 00:07:04.058
Because he comes back to the door and he

00:07:04.058 --> 00:07:07.578
has $20 in his hand, which is like $20,000

00:07:07.598 --> 00:07:09.728
to me on this day, and he presses it into

00:07:09.728 --> 00:07:12.028
my palm and I say, "No, that's too much."

00:07:12.278 --> 00:07:15.487
And he says to me, "My wife died of cancer

00:07:15.487 --> 00:07:19.968
five years ago, and I think this is a

00:07:19.968 --> 00:07:23.837
really bad idea I had." And before I know

00:07:23.837 --> 00:07:25.938
it, I am sitting on the porch with this

00:07:25.938 --> 00:07:28.008
man, and he asks me about what my mother

00:07:28.008 --> 00:07:29.638
died of, and I say breast cancer 'cause

00:07:29.638 --> 00:07:31.557
it's the first one that pops to my mind.

00:07:31.888 --> 00:07:33.268
And he says, "What does your sister have?"

00:07:33.268 --> 00:07:35.108
And I say leukemia, which I don't actually

00:07:35.108 --> 00:07:37.318
know is a cancer, but I know kids get it.

00:07:37.818 --> 00:07:39.328
And he tells me that his wife

00:07:39.328 --> 00:07:41.518
Lisa died of lung cancer and that

00:07:41.518 --> 00:07:43.268
his kids live on the West Coast.

00:07:43.797 --> 00:07:45.888
And he tells me how she struggled

00:07:45.918 --> 00:07:48.517
for two years and how he wanted to

00:07:48.518 --> 00:07:50.728
tell her it was okay to go, but he

00:07:50.728 --> 00:07:53.498
couldn't, and how badly he felt that

00:07:53.498 --> 00:07:55.488
he watched her struggle for so long.

00:07:56.118 --> 00:07:58.258
And I sat on that porch and I listened

00:07:58.258 --> 00:08:00.248
to him and I prayed he wouldn't ask me

00:08:00.248 --> 00:08:02.788
anything, and I just wanted to get off.

00:08:03.457 --> 00:08:05.008
And so finally we were hugging and

00:08:05.008 --> 00:08:07.087
I was walking down the walk with $20

00:08:07.087 --> 00:08:09.057
in my hand and it felt like poison.

00:08:09.848 --> 00:08:11.898
I got to my car and I sat in there

00:08:12.308 --> 00:08:14.218
and I remembered how lonely I felt,

00:08:15.058 --> 00:08:18.378
and then I felt so stupid and small,

00:08:19.358 --> 00:08:20.777
'cause I knew at that moment I

00:08:20.778 --> 00:08:23.238
didn't understand real loneliness.

00:08:23.488 --> 00:08:25.817
I was a 20-year-old kid with friends

00:08:25.818 --> 00:08:28.307
and I was stuck in New Hampshire.

00:08:29.077 --> 00:08:30.668
But I had walked away from a man

00:08:30.678 --> 00:08:32.888
who was in a tiny house with a wife

00:08:32.908 --> 00:08:34.638
that was gone and the kids that were

00:08:34.638 --> 00:08:36.957
gone, and he had a loneliness in

00:08:36.957 --> 00:08:39.058
him that I would never understand.

00:08:40.158 --> 00:08:41.377
And so I put the gas in

00:08:41.377 --> 00:08:43.157
my car and I drove home.

00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:47.938
But he has always been with me.

00:08:49.358 --> 00:08:51.968
I have never left that man on that porch

00:08:51.968 --> 00:08:54.588
that day, and I'm hopeful that I'll

00:08:54.588 --> 00:08:57.057
never be as lonely as he was that day.

00:08:57.598 --> 00:08:57.938
Thank you.

00:09:00.228 --> 00:09:01.587
I say that elephants can change their

00:09:01.587 --> 00:09:03.447
color, meaning the primary stake of a

00:09:03.448 --> 00:09:05.817
story, the primary reason we're invested

00:09:05.817 --> 00:09:07.538
in a character, that can change over time,

00:09:07.548 --> 00:09:09.528
as it did in the story you just heard.

00:09:09.668 --> 00:09:11.698
The initial elephant is will

00:09:11.698 --> 00:09:13.847
Matt find a way to get out of New

00:09:13.848 --> 00:09:15.508
Hampshire with no gas and no money?

00:09:15.967 --> 00:09:17.608
Eventually, that elephant changes colors.

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:19.718
When I end up on the porch in front of

00:09:19.718 --> 00:09:21.757
a blue door with a man who's talking

00:09:21.757 --> 00:09:23.098
to me about the death of his wife,

00:09:23.298 --> 00:09:25.608
it's no longer a concern how I'm

00:09:25.608 --> 00:09:26.798
going to get out of New Hampshire.

00:09:27.158 --> 00:09:28.918
That stake is now gone.

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:29.918
There's a new stake.

00:09:30.117 --> 00:09:31.208
How is Matt going to

00:09:31.208 --> 00:09:32.408
deal with this situation?

00:09:32.608 --> 00:09:33.658
But there has to be that

00:09:33.677 --> 00:09:35.097
elephant in a story all the time.

00:09:35.268 --> 00:09:36.418
There has to be a reason that the

00:09:36.418 --> 00:09:38.448
audience is invested in what is

00:09:38.448 --> 00:09:39.808
happening to the character, the

00:09:39.808 --> 00:09:41.798
product, the company, the CEO.

00:09:41.817 --> 00:09:42.808
Whoever is speaking, there

00:09:42.808 --> 00:09:43.828
has to be an elephant.

00:09:44.008 --> 00:09:45.518
When Steve Jobs takes the stage and says-

00:09:45.698 --> 00:09:48.696
This is a day I've been looking

00:09:48.696 --> 00:09:50.216
forward to for two and a half years.

00:09:51.386 --> 00:09:53.216
"I have been waiting two and a half years

00:09:53.226 --> 00:09:55.016
to tell you this." That's the elephant.

00:09:55.226 --> 00:09:57.516
It is, "I have news that's going

00:09:57.516 --> 00:09:59.236
to blow you away. I have been

00:09:59.236 --> 00:10:00.645
waiting a long time to share this

00:10:00.645 --> 00:10:02.166
with you." That is the elephant.

00:10:02.416 --> 00:10:04.655
We must open our stories with an elephant.

00:10:04.956 --> 00:10:05.795
The other stakes I'm gonna

00:10:05.796 --> 00:10:07.076
discuss, they're all optional,

00:10:07.105 --> 00:10:08.195
but this one is essential.

00:10:08.645 --> 00:10:10.996
We have to have something to invest

00:10:10.996 --> 00:10:12.416
our audience in, to make them feel

00:10:12.416 --> 00:10:13.716
like they are with us and that they

00:10:13.716 --> 00:10:14.916
wanna get to the end to find out

00:10:14.926 --> 00:10:16.335
how things are going to turn out.

00:10:16.645 --> 00:10:17.946
We open with an elephant,

00:10:18.156 --> 00:10:19.546
the first and most important

00:10:19.546 --> 00:10:21.146
stake in every story we tell.

